  "system_description": {
    "narrative_title": "War-driven energy shock: soaring fuel profits, supply risk, social spillovers",
    "narrative_summary": "An open system in which Middle East conflict(s), constrained upstream output decisions (OPEC+), and winter demand intersect with refineries, storage, and shipping to drive crude and refined-fuel price spikes. Those price dynamics transmit to corporate profits and financial flows (buybacks, investor gains) while feeding inflationary pressure, industrial stress, and social unrest in energy-importing economies; security responses and military logistics further couple back into market risk and supply availability. The system continuously exchanges material (oil, refined products, coal, electricity), information (price signals, policy decisions), and influence (military presence, sanctions, strikes) with a larger geopolitical-economic environment.",
    "scope": "International oil and refined-fuels market dynamics (production, OPEC+ quotas, shipping security, refining margins, storage levels), major oil & gas firms' financial responses (profits, buybacks, investment choices), regional gas storage and winter demand in Europe, and short-term social/political reactions in affected economies (strikes, protests, price-sensitive unrest).",
    "super_system": "Global geopolitical-economic-energy security system",
    "adjacent_subsystems": [
      "Global financial markets and investor behavior",
      "Domestic politics and social stability (strikes, protests, wage pressures)",
      "Shipping, logistics, and strategic storage infrastructure"
    ],
    "evidence_headlines": [
      "BP’s profits surge to record on high fuel prices",
      "ExxonMobil beats third-quarter profit estimates on refining boom",
      "OPEC+ set to hold December oil output steady, Reuters reports",
      "Brent 2026 view tops $90 for first time since May as MidEast stability remains elusive",
      "Germany fails to meet gas storage target as winter approaches",
      "Exclusive | Inside the U.S. Military’s Open-Ended Mission to Keep Gulf Oil Flowing"
    ]
  },
